ntent": "You can relate <strong>indirect</strong> <strong>objects</strong> to <strong>direct</strong> <strong>objects</strong> in a sentence, as a way to avoid repetition.&nbsp; "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Today, I'm talking about the implementation issues related to data structures. First, I'm explaining them. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Different variations of fundamental data structures exist if we understand them and know how to use them; then, it is easy to make sure that program design has good quality and avoids failures."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>Simple Present</strong></p> <p>To create an affirmative sentence in the&nbsp;<strong>simple</strong> <strong>present</strong> using <strong>direct</strong> <strong>objects,</strong> you need to use the following structure.</p> <p><strong>Direct</strong> <strong>objects</strong> always follow action verbs that need something or someone to receive the action.</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Pronoun + Verb + <strong>Complement (direct object)</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I want to know more about <strong>data structures. </strong></i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>To create an affirmative sentence in the&nbsp;<strong>simple</strong> <strong>present</strong> using indirect objects, you need to follow the next structure.</p> <p><strong>Indirect</strong> <strong>objects</strong> typically refer to the <strong>direct</strong> <strong>object</strong>.</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Pronoun + Verb + <strong>Indirect object</strong> + Complement</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I want to know more about <strong>data structures </strong>and how we relate <strong>them </strong>to different fields in software programming.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Present Continuous</strong></p> <p>To create an affirmative sentence in the&nbsp;<strong>present</strong> <strong>continuous</strong> using <strong>direct</strong> <strong>objects</strong>, you need to follow the next structure.</p> <p><strong>Direct</strong> <strong>objects</strong> always follow action verbs that need something or someone to receive the action.</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Pronoun + Verb to be + Verb (ing) + <strong>Complement (direct object)</strong></p> <p>&nbsp;Example:</p> <p><i>I am checking all the <strong>issues</strong> with data implementation. </i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>To create an affirmative sentence in the&nbsp;<strong>present</strong> <strong>continuous</strong> using <strong>indirect</strong> <strong>objects</strong>, you need to follow the next structure.</p> <p>Indirect objects typically refer to the direct object.</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Pronoun + Verb to be + Verb (ing) + <strong>Indirect object</strong> + Complement</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I am checking all the <strong>issues</strong> with data implementation because we are finding solutions to <strong>them </strong>in the next meeting.</i></p>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "issue"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the distribution of a group or series"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I'm talking about the implementation issues related to data structures. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n3le02ob07aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "arrange"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to organize"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They are a way of arranging data to be used in a computer program."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n3le02ob07aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "confine "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to put in limits"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In procedural languages as C, the data structure is placed in one part of the program, and the operations are confined s